---
/**
 * Second GEO explainer, same EtG skeleton: anxious mix-up → short answer →
 * myth (the 1,000 W pack) → math table → three scenarios → calculator.
 * Solar-generator-sizing already covers panels. This page is only W vs Wh.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site.ts';
import { getScenario, analyzeScenario, scenarioQuery } from '../../data/scenarios.ts';
import { isCurrent } from '../../lib/catalog.ts';
import db from '../../data/power-stations.json';

const catalog = db.stations.filter(isCurrent);
const cpapDry = getScenario('cpap-overnight');
const fridge = getScenario('fridge-outage');
const tool = getScenario('power-tool-off-grid');
if (!cpapDry || !fridge || !tool) throw new Error('watts-vs-wh scenarios missing');
const dryA = analyzeScenario(cpapDry, catalog);
const fridgeA = analyzeScenario(fridge, catalog);
const toolA = analyzeScenario(tool, catalog);

const n = (value: number) => Math.round(value).toLocaleString('en-US');
const origin = Astro.site ?? site.url;
const path = '/guides/watts-vs-watt-hours/';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between watts and watt-hours?',
    a: 'Watts (W) are how fast energy moves: output. Watt-hours (Wh) are how much is stored: capacity. A 1,000 W station can still hold only 500 Wh, which is about 30 minutes at 1,000 W, not a night.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is 1,000 W enough for a fridge?',
    a: '1,000 W is output, not runtime. A full-size fridge in this catalog runs near 150 W but starts near 1,200 W. You need inverter surge for the start and watt-hours for the hours. One number cannot answer both.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many watt-hours do I need for a CPAP night?',
    a: `A dry CPAP night in this catalog shops about ${n(dryA.plan.recommendedWh)} Wh after inverter loss and reserve. A heated humidifier night is larger. Watts on the box do not size that night.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a higher watt rating mean longer runtime?',
    a: 'No. Higher watts mean the inverter can hold a heavier load. Runtime is capacity divided by the load, after losses. A 2,000 W inverter on a 500 Wh pack is still a short pack.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a solar generator, then?',
    a: 'A battery with an optional solar input. Panel watts are a charge rate in sun, not extra watt-hours in the pack. See how to size a solar generator for that split.',
  },
];

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Watts vs watt-hours on a portable power station',
  description:
    'Watts are output. Watt-hours are stored energy. A 1,000 W rating does not mean a 1,000 Wh night. Worked numbers for a CPAP, a fridge, and a saw.',
  url: new URL(path, origin).href,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: site.name },
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: site.publisherName },
};
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<Base
  title="Watts vs watt-hours on a power station"
  description="Watts are output. Watt-hours are capacity. A 1,000 W rating is not a 1,000 Wh night. Worked calculator numbers for a CPAP, a fridge, and a saw."
  canonicalPath={path}
  ogImage="/images/guides/watts-vs-watt-hours/cover.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">Sizing basics</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-4xl">
      Watts vs watt-hours: which number actually sizes the station
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      The listing says 1,000 W. You need the fridge overnight. Those are not the same unit, and
      mixing them is how people buy a pack that trips on the first compressor start, or dies in 40
      minutes.
    </p>
    <figure class="mt-8">
      <img
        src="/images/guides/watts-vs-watt-hours/cover.png"
        alt="Pencil sketch of a storage tank next to a faucet, the watt-hour tank versus the watts rate"
        width="1024"
        height="1024"
        class="w-full h-auto rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-ink-200 dark:border-ink-800"
      />
    </figure>

    <aside class="mt-8 rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-ink-200 bg-white p-5 text-sm dark:border-ink-800 dark:bg-ink-900">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink-900 dark:text-ink-100">Editorial note</p>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Maintained by {site.name}. Hours and watt-hours below come from the same formulas as the
        <a href="/calculator/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">calculator</a>.
        Device watts are engineering ranges. Not a lab test of your machine.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-500">
        <a href="/methodology/" class="underline">Methodology</a>: inverter 0.85, reserve 1.20, usable capacity 0.85.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The short answer</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Watts (W) tell you whether the inverter can hold the load. Watt-hours (Wh) tell you how long
        the battery lasts. You need both. A third check, inverter surge, decides motor starts. A
        single "1,000 W portable power station" headline answers none of those by itself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What each number is</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Watts are a rate. A 60 W bulb uses 60 joules per second. Run it for ten hours and you have
        used 600 Wh. The station's output rating is the ceiling on that rate. The capacity rating is
        the tank.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        On a spec sheet, continuous watts are the running ceiling. Peak watts are either inverter
        surge or a resistive boost. Capacity is watt-hours. This site checks them separately so a
        large tank cannot hide a weak inverter, and a loud watt number cannot hide an empty tank.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The 1,000 W pack is misleading</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Retail titles often lead with watts because the number looks bigger. Search "1000W power
        station" and you will see packs from a few hundred watt-hours to well over 1,000 Wh sharing
        the same headline wattage.
      </p>
      <h3 class="mt-6 text-base font-semibold">The context behind "1,000 W"</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        1,000 W means the inverter can hold about a 1,000 W running load. It does not mean you have
        1,000 Wh in the battery. A 1,000 W / 500 Wh pack at a 1,000 W load lasts on the order of
        half an hour before losses, not a night. The same 1,000 W inverter on a 1,024 Wh pack is a
        different purchase.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        For a fridge, even 1,000 W continuous can be the wrong gate. The start in this catalog is
        about 1,200 W of inverter surge. Matching the running watts is not the same check.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How the hours fall out</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Roughly: watt-hours in the tank, times 0.85 usable, divided by the duty-weighted watts.
        Then compare continuous watts to the load, and inverter surge to the largest motor start.
        The
        <a href="/calculator/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">calculator</a>
        names which of those three fails first.
      </p>
      <figure class="mt-6">
        <img
          src="/images/guides/watts-vs-watt-hours/infographic.png"
          alt="Three loads: CPAP is a watt-hour problem, a home fridge needs surge and watt-hours, a saw is gated by surge"
          width="1024"
          height="1024"
          class="w-full h-auto rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-ink-200 dark:border-ink-800"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-500">
          CPAP shops about 542 Wh. Fridge about 826 Wh plus a 1,200 W start. Saw about 572 Wh with a 2,400 W start.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-ink-200 dark:border-ink-800">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse bg-white text-left text-sm dark:bg-ink-900">
          <caption class="sr-only">Worked loads showing watts versus watt-hours</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-ink-200 dark:border-ink-800">
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Load</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Running / start</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Shop about</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-ink-100 dark:border-ink-800/60">
              <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">CPAP, humidifier off, 8 h</th>
              <td class="tabular px-4 py-3">{n(dryA.sizing.simultaneousW)} W / no start modelled</td>
              <td class="tabular px-4 py-3">{n(dryA.plan.recommendedWh)} Wh</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-ink-100 dark:border-ink-800/60">
              <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Home fridge outage, 8 h</th>
              <td class="tabular px-4 py-3">{n(fridgeA.sizing.simultaneousW)} W / {n(fridgeA.sizing.peakSurgeW)} W start</td>
              <td class="tabular px-4 py-3">{n(fridgeA.plan.recommendedWh)} Wh</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Circular saw, bursts</th>
              <td class="tabular px-4 py-3">{n(toolA.sizing.simultaneousW)} W / {n(toolA.sizing.peakSurgeW)} W start</td>
              <td class="tabular px-4 py-3">{n(toolA.plan.recommendedWh)} Wh</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink-500">
        Shop figures include inverter loss and reserve. The saw is a surge problem. The CPAP is a
        watt-hour problem. The fridge is both.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Three ways the mix-up shows up</h2>
      <h3 class="mt-6 text-base font-semibold">Scenario 1: CPAP night</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Draw is small, on the order of {n(dryA.sizing.simultaneousW)} W dry. Almost any mid-size
        inverter holds it. The number that decides the purchase is watt-hours for eight hours, and
        whether the humidifier is on. See
        <a href="/guides/cpap-backup-power/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">CPAP backup</a>.
      </p>
      <h3 class="mt-6 text-base font-semibold">Scenario 2: Home fridge</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Running watts look modest. Startup is about {n(fridgeA.sizing.peakSurgeW)} W. Capacity around
        {n(fridgeA.plan.recommendedWh)} Wh covers eight hours in this catalog only if the peak is
        inverter surge.
        <a href="/guides/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">
          Can a power station run a refrigerator?
        </a>
      </p>
      <h3 class="mt-6 text-base font-semibold">Scenario 3: A saw</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Capacity can be small because the tool runs in bursts. The start is about {n(toolA.sizing.peakSurgeW)} W.
        Extra watt-hours do not pull the trigger. A boost rating does not either.
        <a href="/scenarios/power-tool-off-grid/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">Power tool scenario</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What will not help</h2>
      <h3 class="mt-6 text-base font-semibold">Buying the louder watt number</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        2,000 W continuous on a 500 Wh pack is still 500 Wh. You get headroom, not hours.
      </p>
      <h3 class="mt-6 text-base font-semibold">Treating panel watts as extra battery</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        A 400 W panel is a charge rate in sun, after angle and weather. It is not 400 Wh sitting in
        the case. That split is on
        <a href="/guides/solar-generator-sizing/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">how to size a solar generator</a>.
      </p>
      <h3 class="mt-6 text-base font-semibold">Ignoring surge because capacity looks big</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        A 2 kWh pack with a boost-only peak still fails a fridge start on this site.
        <a href="/guides/surge-vs-boost/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">Surge versus boost</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-6">
        {
          faq.map((item, index) => (
            <details class="border-b border-ink-200 py-5 dark:border-ink-800" open={index === 0}>
              <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">{item.q}</summary>
              <p class="mt-3 max-w-[72ch] text-base leading-7 text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">{item.a}</p>
            </details>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Related reading</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/solar-generator-sizing/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">How to size a solar generator</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/how-long-will-a-1000wh-power-station-last/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">How long will a 1000Wh power station last?</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">Can a portable power station run a refrigerator?</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/surge-vs-boost/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">Inverter surge versus boost</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/cpap-backup-power/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">CPAP backup</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/methodology/" class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300">Methodology</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 rounded-[var(--radius-card)] border border-ink-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-ink-800 dark:bg-ink-900">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Calculate your own estimate</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-sm text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
        Add the devices you actually run. The first line of the result says whether you are short on
        watt-hours, continuous watts, or startup surge.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-2 sm:flex-row">
        <a
          href={`/calculator/#calculator?load=${encodeURIComponent(scenarioQuery(cpapDry))}&days=1`}
          class="press inline-flex justify-center rounded-[var(--radius-control)] bg-moss-700 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-moss-600"
        >
          CPAP night
        </a>
        <a
          href={`/calculator/#calculator?load=${encodeURIComponent(scenarioQuery(fridge))}&days=1`}
          class="press inline-flex justify-center rounded-[var(--radius-control)] border border-ink-200 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium hover:border-moss-500 dark:border-ink-700"
        >
          Fridge outage
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <Disclaimer electrical />
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
